Thiazide Diuretic Mismanagement
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Renal Loss
Insidious lethargy and confusion, Pronounced orthostatic hypotension, Marked hyponatremia and hypokalemia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-046
Reason for Visit: Progressive confusion, severe generalized weakness, and frequent fainting in an elderly patient, requiring urgent evaluation for severe electrolyte derangement and hypovolemia secondary to over-diuresis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an elderly patient recently commenced on a high dose of thiazide diuretic who has become progressively confused over three days and suffered a syncopal fall today. This mandated an urgent after-hours assessment to evaluate for life-threatening hyponatremia and severe intravascular volume depletion prior to standard morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Severe exhaustion, severe dizziness when standing, and acute confusion. Adult child states, He has completely lost his energy, he is talking nonsense about things that happened years ago, and he collapsed in the hallway this afternoon when he stood up.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have developed with an insidious onset over the past five days. The patient exhibits severe lethargy, confusion, and generalized fatigue. Initially, the patient experienced pronounced polyuria after starting the thiazide diuretic, which has now transitioned to oliguria as significant volume depletion has ensued. The patient complains of severe, painful muscle cramps in the legs. Today, pronounced orthostatic hypotension led to a near-syncope event and a subsequent fall in the hallway. The patient complains of a profoundly dry mouth and intense thirst. An acute, painful swelling of the right first metatarsophalangeal joint is noted, indicating an acute gout flare likely precipitated by hyperuricemia.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; currently bedbound due to severe weakness and fear of falling.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent syncope, traumatic fracture, and progression to hyponatremic seizures; adult child is providing continuous supervision.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg (Supine) -> 90/55 mmHg (Standing) | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 72.0 Kg
General: Appears frail, chronically dehydrated, disoriented to time, and lethargic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked. A profound orthostatic drop in blood pressure is objectively confirmed upon assisted standing, directly reproducing the patient's severe dizziness. Neurological: The diagnostic triad is clinically confirmed. There is insidious lethargy and confusion (Glasgow Coma Scale 13), pronounced orthostatic hypotension, and clinical signs highly suspicious for marked hyponatremia and hypokalemia (muscle cramps, profound weakness). Integumentary: Examination reveals decreased skin turgor, particularly evident on the clavicle or sternum (tenting >3 seconds). The oral mucous membranes are profoundly dry with decreased salivary pooling. Musculoskeletal: Right first MTP joint is erythematous, hot, and exquisitely tender (acute gout).
Environmental Observations: Fall hazards removed by adult child.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient was commenced on Hydrochlorothiazide 25mg daily one week ago; currently taking alongside concurrent ACE inhibitors.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Thiazide Diuretic Mismanagement - Status: Acute Decompensation
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Global, progressive encephalopathy and confusion in the setting of massive fluid loss, completely lacking acute, focal, lateralizing neurological deficits (e.g., unilateral weakness or facial droop).

Sepsis (Urinary or Respiratory) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, rigors, productive cough, or dysuria, alongside a clear pharmacological trigger for the volume loss.

Dementia Progression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, hyper-rapid onset of confusion correlating perfectly with the initiation of the diuretic and objective signs of massive physical dehydration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits severe, decompensated volume depletion and profound symptomatic hyponatremia secondary to iatrogenic thiazide diuretic mismanagement. The intense natriuresis has dragged massive amounts of free water from the intravascular space, leading to orthostatic syncope. Concurrently, the severe sodium depletion has caused acute metabolic encephalopathy, manifesting as lethargy and confusion. Delaying assessment until standard in-hours would have carried a critical risk of the patient developing fatal hyponatremic seizures or suffering a catastrophic fracture from a syncopal fall. Urgent after-hours assessment was vital to immediately cease the offending medication, ensure strict bed rest to prevent falls, and initiate very gradual, cautious oral rehydration prior to urgent morning laboratory analysis. The expected investigation results include marked hyponatremia with concurrent hypokalemia, hypercalcemia, and a pre-renal azotemia pattern on serum biochemistry that would assist in confirming the diagnosis formally.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Mandated the immediate, absolute cessation of Hydrochlorothiazide. Instructed the patient to remain strictly supine or seated. Advised cautious, slow oral rehydration with isotonic fluids, specifically avoiding massive boluses of plain free water to prevent precipitating central pontine myelinolysis or worsening the acute hyponatremia. Prescribed Colchicine 500mcg PO twice daily for acute gout management.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely restore your blood pressure without shocking your brain, commit to drinking exactly half a glass of electrolyte fluid every hour, no more. Use a timer. When you successfully follow this slow pacing and notice your confusion slightly lifting, explicitly acknowledge that your deliberate patience is allowing your brain to adjust safely, reinforcing your strict adherence to the slow schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review first thing tomorrow morning for a comprehensive metabolic panel (UEC, Calcium, Uric Acid) and formal medication rationalization.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000 if the patient experiences a seizure, if they become completely unarousable, or if they experience a complete loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a complex medication toxicity causing severe salt imbalances in the blood, requiring formal laboratory analysis to guide correction safely.
Clinical Course: The new blood pressure pill forced the kidneys to dump massive amounts of salt and water. This dropped the blood pressure and critically lowered the salt levels surrounding the brain cells, causing the confusion. Stopping the pill and cautiously replacing fluids will slowly reverse this.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to rapidly force liters of plain water into the patient. Because the blood sodium is critically low, forcing plain water can cause the brain to swell massively, leading to fatal seizures. Hydration must be slow and steady.

